434886932373047, 0.0038280487060546875, 0.004634857177734375, 0.021881103515625, 0.0255279541015625, -0.0255889892578125, -0.0188140869140625, 0.01739501953125, -0.016845703125, -0.01922607421875, -0.006107330322265625, 0.00936126708984375, 0.0059814453125, 0.0093231201171875, -0.02374267578125, 0.004070281982421875, 0.038360595703125, 0.0256805419921875, -0.0009512901306152344, -0.0256805419921875, -0.006656646728515625, -0.0030040740966796875, 0.0175323486328125, -0.011627197265625, 0.0029430389404296875, -0.005649566650390625, -0.031219482421875, 0.005878448486328125, 0.0043182373046875, 0.004673004150390625, -0.0079193115234375, 0.03460693359375, 0.022918701171875, 0.006595611572265625, -0.02392578125, 0.006244659423828125, 0.0165863037109375, 0.0024280548095703125, 0.0026397705078125, 0.009368896484375, -0.0209503173828125, 0.01076507568359375, -0.018035888671875, -0.01505279541015625, -0.0237579345703125, -0.01523590087890625, 0.01549530029296875, -0.052581787109375, -0.0010728836059570312, -0.0306549072265625, -0.0215301513671875, 0.0166015625, -0.025360107421875, -0.00916290283203125, -0.0084991455078125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Memory-based self-improving agents\u2014those that learn from an online stream of tasks and improve over time by maintaining a textual memory bank\u2014have shown great promise in recent literature [1,2,3]. However, the reliability aspects of these methods have been critically overlooked. In this work, we address the following research questions, structured for clarity:\n\n1. **Robustness:** To what extent do memory-based self-improving agents exhibit robustness\u2014defined as low performance variance and resistance to failure modes (e.g., catastrophic forgetting, spurious memory entries, abrupt performance drops)\u2014when exposed to stochasticity and changes in task order?\n2. **Fragility Factors:** What specific factors contribute to their observed fragility (i.e., susceptibility to performance degradation or failure under perturbations such as task order shuffling or noisy feedback)?\n3. **Evaluation Protocols:** How can evaluation protocols be improved to better capture these reliability issues?\n\nFor example, a fragile agent might suddenly fail previously mastered tasks after learning a new one (catastrophic forgetting), or record misleading information in its memory (spurious entries) that harms future performance.\n\n**Background.** We focus on two representative memory-based methods:\n- **Episodic Memory Augmented Agents (EMAA) [4]:** Store and retrieve textual summaries of past episodes to inform future decisions, emphasizing episodic recall.\n- **Self-Reflective Language Agents (SRLA) [5]:** Maintain a log of self-generated reflections and lessons learned after each task, focusing on meta-cognitive self-assessment.\n\nThese methods were selected because they exemplify two contrasting approaches to memory construction\u2014episodic versus reflective\u2014and are widely used as baselines in recent benchmarks [6,7]. While these two agents do not exhaust the space of memory-based architectures, they capture key differences in memory structure and content that are hypothesized to affect robustness and reliability. We acknowledge that findings may not generalize to all agent types and discuss this limitation further below. Prior evaluations have primarily used fixed task sequences, often without reporting variance or sensitivity to task order, leaving open questions about generalizability and reproducibility.\n\n**Methods.** We conduct a comprehensive re-evaluation of EMAA and SRLA along two axes:\n1. **Performance Variance:** Running each agent on five independent random seeds to quantify performance variance and identify failure modes.\n2. **Task Order Sensitivity:** Randomly shuffling the order of tasks to assess the effect of task order.\n\nThe task suite consists of 10 multi-step reasoning and manipulation tasks, selected to ensure diversity in required skills, environment feedback, and complexity. Tasks are:\n\n- **Symbolic Reasoning Tasks:**\n  - Text-based logic puzzles [8]\n  - Arithmetic word problems [9]\n  - Sequence prediction [10]\n  - Symbolic Sorting (custom)\n  - Rule Induction (custom)\n- **Embodied Manipulation Tasks:**\n  - Object arrangement [11]\n  - Navigation [12]\n  - Sorting in simulated 3D environments [13]\n  - Object Retrieval (custom)\n  - Spatial Reasoning (custom)\n\nCustom tasks were designed to fill gaps in coverage (e.g., sorting and spatial reasoning not present in prior benchmarks) and are described in Supplementary Table 1. Inclusion criteria were based on coverage of both symbolic and embodied domains and prior use in benchmarking literature. While we aimed for relevance to real-world scenarios, we note that the custom tasks are simplified abstractions and may not capture all complexities of real environments.\n\nFor each experiment, task order was randomized by sampling a single random permutation per run (rather than exhaustively enumerating all permutations, due to combinatorial explosion). We recognize that this approach may not capture the full variability of possible task sequences; however, it provides a tractable compromise between coverage and computational feasibility. Alternative strategies, such as stratified sampling or increasing the number of permutations, could be explored in future work to further assess order effects.\n\nAgent hyperparameters were set to default values as reported in the original papers: memory size = 100 entries, retrieval strategy = cosine similarity over sentence embeddings, learning rate = 1e-4, batch size = 16, and no additional tuning was performed.\n\n**Memory Analysis Rubric.** For memory analysis, we manually inspect the agents\u2019 memory banks after each run, using a rubric that codes for specificity (level of detail, e.g., explicit mention of actions and outcomes), relevance (applicability to future tasks, e.g., generalizable strategies), and completeness (coverage of key events, e.g., whether all critical steps are recorded). Rubric items were developed based on a review of prior work on memory evaluation and piloted on a subset of memory entries to ensure clarity and coverage. An example rubric item: \u201cDoes the memory entry specify both the task goal and the outcome of the attempted solution?\u201d Two independent annotators performed the coding, and inter-annotator agreement was measured using Cohen\u2019s kappa (average \u03ba = 0.81). Disagreements were resolved through discussion until consensus was reached in all cases. See Supplementary Table 2 for the full rubric and development process.\n\n**Results.** Our experiments reveal two key findings that expose the fragility of current methods:\n\n1. **Evaluation Noise and Failure Modes:** Agent evaluation is inherently noisy in complex environments and on multi-step tasks. Across five runs, mean final task success rates ranged from 62% to 78% (95% confidence intervals \u00b16%), with standard deviation in final task success rates ranging from 8% to 15%. For example, in one run, an EMAA agent failed three previously solved tasks after encountering a novel navigation task (catastrophic forgetting). In another, an SRLA agent recorded a memory entry stating \u201cSorting objects by color is always optimal,\u201d which led to repeated failures on tasks requiring sorting by size (spurious memory entry). Stacking a self-improving loop on top can further amplify this noise, occasionally leading to such failure modes.\n\n2. **Task Order Dependence:** The agent\u2019s improvement is highly dependent on task order. In some permutations, agents failed to improve on later tasks, with average performance drops of up to 20% (mean drop: 13% \u00b14% CI, p < 0.05, paired t-test) compared to the default curriculum order. Prior works often adopt default orderings that impose an implicit curriculum, acting as a hidden prerequisite for success.\n\nManual examination of the agents\u2019 memory indicates that task and environment underspecification contribute to this fragility: memory entries often lack sufficient detail to generalize across task variations, and ambiguous environment feedback leads to misleading or incomplete memories. For example, 37% (\u00b15% CI) of memory entries in the baseline condition omitted explicit task goals, and 22% (\u00b14% CI) failed to record outcomes.\n\n**Intervention:** To validate this, we introduced a more detailed memory rubric (requiring explicit mention of task goals, actions, and outcomes in each entry) and provided agents with structured environment feedback (e.g., explicit success/failure signals and clarifying messages). This intervention was operationalized by modifying the memory-writing module to enforce rubric compliance using template-based prompts (e.g., \u201cTask: [goal]. Action: [steps taken]. Outcome: [result]. Lesson: [reflection].\u201d) and updating the environment API to emit standardized feedback messages (e.g., \u201cSuccess: Object sorted by color,\u201d \u201cFailure: Incorrect navigation path\u201d). Implementation details and code are provided in Supplementary Material. While these changes improved memory quality and reduced ambiguity, they required manual engineering and may not generalize to all agent architectures or task domains without further adaptation. There is a risk of overfitting agent behavior to the rubric or structured feedback, potentially reducing generalizability to less-structured or novel environments. The impact was measured by comparing performance degradation in shuffled task orders before and after the intervention: average degradation reduced from 20% to 10% (mean post-intervention: 8% \u00b13% CI, p < 0.05), but significant gaps still remain.\n\n**Discussion.** These persistent gaps suggest that other factors\u2014such as limitations in memory retrieval mechanisms, insufficient exploration, or unmodeled environment stochasticity\u2014may also contribute to fragility. Limitations of our study include:\n- The scalability of manual annotation (which may not be feasible for larger-scale deployments).\n- Potential biases in task selection (as our suite may not capture the full diversity of real-world tasks).\n- The focus on only two agent architectures, which limits the generalizability of our findings.\n- The possibility that enforcing a strict memory rubric may overfit agent behavior to the evaluation criteria, reducing generalizability.\n\nSome findings, such as the high variance in performance and the impact of task order, are robust across seeds and agents. However, the effectiveness of rubric-based interventions and structured feedback is preliminary and may not extend to other architectures or domains without further validation. Future work should:\n- Systematically ablate retrieval strategies, vary exploration parameters, or introduce controlled environment randomness to isolate their effects.\n- Test hypotheses such as \u201cretrieval failures disproportionately affect transfer to novel tasks\u201d or \u201cincreased environment stochasticity amplifies memory errors\u201d in targeted experiments.\n- Expand evaluation to a broader set of agent architectures.\n- Develop and pilot automated or semi-automated memory analysis tools to improve scalability and consistency.\n\n**Recommendations.** We advocate for more rigorous evaluation protocols for self-improving agents, including:\n1. Reporting mean and variance of results across multiple random seeds.\n2. Systematically varying and reporting task order effects.\n3. Providing statistical significance tests (e.g., mean \u00b1 confidence intervals, p-values) for all key comparisons, with clear reporting of test assumptions and corrections for multiple comparisons where applicable.\n4. Offering detailed breakdowns of failure cases with standardized taxonomies (e.g., catastrophic forgetting, spurious memory, retrieval failure).\n\nFor effective human oversight, we recommend developing user interfaces that allow users to audit, edit, and flag agent memories, with features such"}}